14:message/user:
    You are writing a CONTEXT CHECKPOINT. The conversation so far is about to be replaced by your summary: a...ou read — will be gone unless you write it down here. [hash=61ccdd89606edae3]

    Answer with text only; do not call tools.

    First think in an <analysis> block: walk through the conversation in order and note, for each part, what... The analysis is discarded; only the summary is kept. [hash=6c8fe7f42cd87772]

    Then write the summary in a <summary> block with these sections (leave a section out only when it has nothing):

    1. Goal: what the user wants overall, and what they asked for most recently.
    2. User preferences and corrections: constraints, rules and feedback to keep following (e.g. "run only t...sts", "never commit X"). Quote short phrases exactly. [hash=ee22b36d680a7ff6]
    3. Key facts: technical concepts, decisions taken and their reasons, versions, configuration, things checked and found true or false.
    4. Files and code: each file read, changed or created — why it matters, what changed, and any short snip...xt model would otherwise have to re-read to continue. [hash=433d036db8f41454]
    5. Errors and fixes: what failed, the cause, the fix, and anything still unresolved.
    6. Done so far: work that is finished and verified (say how it was verified).
    7. Still to do: tasks the user asked for that are not finished, in order.
    8. Where it stopped: precisely what was being worked on right before this checkpoint — the file, the command, the state it was left in.
    9. Next step: the single next action, and only if it follows from the user's latest request; quote the u...k was finished, say so instead of inventing new work. [hash=0e78fd8ce329a23b]

    Be precise and complete rather than short: concrete names and values beat general descriptions. Write in the language the user writes in.


